sections.body:
Before Java 7, closing a resource safely meant a specific, error-prone dance: open it, then wrap the usage in try/finally, then null-check it inside finally before calling close(), because the open itself might have failed partway through.

```java
BufferedReader reader = null;
try {
    reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path));
    return reader.readLine();
} finally {
    if (reader != null) {
        reader.close();   // close() can ALSO throw IOException
    }
}
```

This has two real problems, not just extra typing. First, the null check exists only because the resource's own constructor might have thrown before `reader` was ever assigned -- forgetting it risks a `NullPointerException` inside `finally` itself, on top of whatever originally went wrong. Second, and more subtly damaging: if the `try` block throws an exception (say, a genuine I/O failure while reading), and `close()` inside `finally` *also* throws while cleaning up, the exception from `close()` replaces the original one that was already propagating -- the real root cause of the failure is silently discarded, and whoever eventually catches the exception sees only the (usually less informative) failure from `close()`.

Java 7 introduced a form of `try` that declares one or more resources in parentheses right after the keyword; each declared resource is automatically closed at the end of the block -- whether the block completes normally or exits via an exception -- with no `finally` needed at all for that purpose.

```java
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(path))) {
    return reader.readLine();
}   // reader.close() is called automatically here, no matter how the block exits
```

Any type used this way must implement `java.lang.AutoCloseable` (or its subinterface `Closeable`, used throughout the I/O classes, which narrows `close()`'s declared exception to specifically `IOException`) -- the compiler enforces this, refusing to compile a try-with-resources statement over a type that doesn't implement it.

Multiple resources can be declared in the same statement, separated by semicolons, and they're closed **in reverse order of declaration**:

```java
try (var connection = openConnection();
     var statement = connection.createStatement()) {
    // use statement, which depends on connection being open
}   // statement is closed FIRST, then connection -- reverse of declaration order
```

This ordering isn't arbitrary: a resource declared later often depends on one declared earlier still being open while it's itself being torn down (here, closing a `Statement` while its underlying `Connection` is already closed could misbehave), so closing in reverse mirrors the natural dependency direction, tearing down the most-recently-acquired, most-dependent resource first.

try-with-resources doesn't just automate the close() call -- it also fixes the lost-exception problem from manual cleanup. If the try body throws an exception, *and* the automatic close() call that follows also throws, the exception from the body is treated as the **primary** exception and is the one actually propagated to the caller. The exception from close() is not discarded -- it's attached to the primary exception as a **suppressed exception**, retrievable by calling `getSuppressed()` on the primary exception, which returns an array of any such attached exceptions.

```java
class FlakyResource implements AutoCloseable {
    void doWork() { throw new RuntimeException("work failed"); }
    public void close() { throw new IllegalStateException("close failed too"); }
}

try (FlakyResource r = new FlakyResource()) {
    r.doWork();
} catch (RuntimeException e) {
    System.out.println("primary: " + e.getMessage());              // "work failed"
    System.out.println("suppressed: " + e.getSuppressed()[0].getMessage()); // "close failed too"
}
```

If the try body completes *without* throwing, but close() throws on its own, that close() exception is simply the one that propagates -- there's no suppression involved in that simpler case, since there's no earlier exception it would need to defer to.

Implementing `AutoCloseable` requires exactly one method: `void close() throws Exception` (a concrete class is free to narrow the declared exception, including declaring none at all, since overriding methods may narrow -- but never widen -- their declared checked exceptions).

```java
class SimpleConnection implements AutoCloseable {
    private final String name;

    SimpleConnection(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("opening " + name);
    }

    void query(String sql) {
        System.out.println(name + " running: " + sql);
    }

    @Override
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("closing " + name);
    }
}

try (SimpleConnection conn = new SimpleConnection("db1")) {
    conn.query("SELECT 1");
}
// prints: opening db1 / db1 running: SELECT 1 / closing db1
```

One more useful detail, added in Java 9: the resource in a try-with-resources statement no longer has to be a *new* declaration inside the parentheses -- it can be any existing, effectively final variable already in scope: `try (conn) { ... }`. The resource must still be effectively final (never reassigned after its initial assignment), for the same reason lambdas require effectively final captured variables -- Java needs a single, unambiguous value to close, decided once, not a variable that might have been pointed somewhere else by the time the block ends.

examples.code:
// BEFORE: manual cleanup, with a null check needed and a lost-exception risk
BufferedReader reader = null;
try {
    reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"));
    System.out.println(reader.readLine());
} finally {
    if (reader != null) {
        reader.close();   // if this throws, it can silently replace an exception from readLine()
    }
}

// AFTER: try-with-resources handles both problems automatically
try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader("data.txt"))) {
    System.out.println(reader.readLine());
}

class Resource implements AutoCloseable {
    private final String name;
    Resource(String name) {
        this.name = name;
        System.out.println("open " + name);
    }
    @Override
    public void close() {
        System.out.println("close " + name);
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (Resource a = new Resource("A");
             Resource b = new Resource("B");
             Resource c = new Resource("C")) {
            System.out.println("using A, B, C");
        }
    }
}

class FlakyResource implements AutoCloseable {
    void doWork() {
        throw new RuntimeException("work failed");
    }
    @Override
    public void close() {
        throw new IllegalStateException("close failed too");
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        try (FlakyResource r = new FlakyResource()) {
            r.doWork();
        } catch (RuntimeException e) {
            System.out.println("caught: " + e.getMessage());
            for (Throwable suppressed : e.getSuppressed()) {
                System.out.println("  suppressed: " + suppressed.getMessage());
            }
        }
    }
}

